* 28 Oct 26 dm  S.88 L1 (R-a): the op pending, the program's own wait asking whether a resize was read by a dialogue and not delivered (the note
*               $sl_key keeps in common /slpend/); open and close forget the note.  The block /slscr/ is unchanged (CPROC declares it too).
* 3 Nov 26 dm  S.113 (owner: "All pages"): the ops draw, peek, flush, unsync, refresh and toolkit; the kept context has the terminal's picture
*               (SL.S.TERM, SL.S.TATTR); get flushes a page of the toolkit; a page that is resized is unsynced and repainted once, at its next flush.
*               The note that the terminal is not known is in a block of its own, /slsync/, so that /slscr/ is unchanged.  put does not write the
*               cursor for a statement that drew by difference and wrote nothing (it is owed to the next flush, /slcurq/, shared with $sl_curat).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $sl_scr(op, scr)
*
*     resized  scr returns 1 if the kept context has been made a new size (the terminal was resized, or TERM w,h
*            typed) since this was last asked, else 0, and the flag is cleared (SCREEN REFRESH's RETURNING)
*     pending  scr returns 1 if $sl_key has handed out the key resize since the program's own wait (SCREEN KEY, SCREEN WAIT) last
*            delivered one, else 0, and the note is cleared.  The wait asks it before it reads a key and delivers resize first: a
*            resize read by a dialogue (a menu, a message box, SCREEN ASK, a pick window, the toolkit's confirm and AI boxes, the
*            IDE's name box) redraws the dialogue and was never the program's, which went on with the old geometry (R-a, 28 Oct 2026).
*            The wait that delivers a resize it read itself asks it too, so that the note is not delivered a second time.
*     get    scr is the screen context (sl.h) the library is to use.  If the library owns the screen and the
*            terminal is no longer the size the kept context was made for, the context is first made the new
*            size, keeping every cell that still fits, and the screen drawn again from it (a page of the toolkit: at its next flush).
*            On a screen the toolkit took (SL.S.TK) the terminal is first brought up to the shadow (flush, below), so that a dialogue or a
*            widget that draws now starts from a terminal that shows the page.  Until SCREEN OPEN has been done
*            it is a blank one of the terminal's size, made afresh on every call and not kept
*            (SL.S.OWNED 0): a pop-up such as a message box can then put back only blanks, where the
*            program had text.  After SCREEN OPEN it is the kept one (SL.S.OWNED 1).
*     peek   (S.113) as get, without the flush: SCREEN KEY after a resize, SCREEN CLOSE.
*     draw   (S.113) as peek, for SCREEN TEXT, SCREEN BOX and SCREEN CLEAR: on a screen the toolkit took, scr<SL.S.DIFF> is 1 (only the cells
*            that differ from what the terminal shows are written; a clear is not written at all) or 2 while the terminal is not known (nothing
*            is written; the next flush repaints).  Everywhere else 0, as get: a user's own program may have printed over its text.
*     put    stores scr back as the kept context if there is one, and puts the terminal's cursor where
*            the context says it is (SL.S.CUR), shown or hidden as SL.S.CURON says.  Nothing to do
*            for a transient context.  (S.113) A context taken with draw whose statement wrote nothing did not move the terminal's
*            cursor: the cursor is then owed (common /slcurq/) and the next flush places it, unless $sl_curat has placed it since.
*     flush  (S.113) the program's own wait (SCREEN KEY, SCREEN WAIT) calls it before it reads a key.  When the terminal is not known
*            (unsync, below) the whole screen is cleared and written again from the shadow ($sl_repaint), once.  Else, on a screen the
*            toolkit took, every row whose shadow is not what the terminal shows has the cells that differ written.  The cursor is put
*            back where the program left it.
*     unsync (S.113) the terminal may not show what the library believes (the cursor lock's warning was drawn over it): the next flush
*            repaints.  Nothing for a screen that is not open.
*     refresh (S.113) Ctrl-L or F5 was read on a page of the toolkit: as unsync, on a screen the toolkit took; nothing on any other.
*     toolkit (S.113) $tk_open marks the screen it took (SL.S.TK).
*     open   makes the kept context: the terminal's size, every cell blank and style n, the cursor at
*            0,0 and shown, the terminal's picture blank.  scr returns it.  The terminal is not touched.
*     close  forgets it.
*
*   The routines of the SCREEN statement ask for the context here and hand it back with put, so
*   the program never handles one.  SED builds its own and calls the widget routines directly.
*
*   WHY THE FLUSH IS ONLY AT THE PROGRAM'S OWN WAIT AND IN GET.  A dialogue takes a COPY of the kept context with get and hands it back with
*   put when it ends.  A flush while such a copy is out would write the page over the dialogue, and the copy would then be put back
*   believing the dialogue is still shown.  The program's wait and a get are where no copy is out.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine sl_scr(op, scr)
$catalog $sl_scr

$include sl.h

   common /slscr/ ctx, ctx.open, ctx.resized
   common /slpend/ rz.pending                     ;* $sl_key's note of a resize handed out and not yet delivered by the program's wait
   common /slsync/ ss.lost                        ;* S.113: 1 when the terminal may not show the kept context (the next flush repaints); a block starts as 0
   common /slcurq/ cq.last, cq.owed               ;* S.113: who placed the terminal's cursor last ('put' or 'curat', $sl_curat), and whether a put left it owed

   if unassigned(ctx.open) then
      ctx = ''
      ctx.open = 0
   end
   if unassigned(ss.lost) then ss.lost = 0

   begin case
      case op = 'get' or op = 'peek' or op = 'draw'
         if ctx.open then
            if ctx<SL.S.W> # @crtwide or ctx<SL.S.H> # @crthigh then gosub resize
            if op = 'get' and ctx<SL.S.TK> then gosub flush
            scr = ctx
            if ss.lost then scr<SL.S.SYNC> = 0
            scr<SL.S.DIFF> = 0                  ;* $sl_form turns it on for the length of one of its calls; draw, on a page of the toolkit, below
            if op = 'draw' and ctx<SL.S.TK> then
               scr<SL.S.DIFF> = (if scr<SL.S.SYNC> = 1 then 1 else 2)
            end
         end else
            gosub blank
            scr = blank.scr
         end

      case op = 'put'
         if ctx.open then
            if scr<SL.S.W> # ctx<SL.S.W> or scr<SL.S.H> # ctx<SL.S.H> then ctx.resized = 1   ;* a dialogue was resized under it
            * S.113: a statement that drew by difference and wrote nothing (its terminal picture is as it was) did not move the terminal's cursor; the
            *   cursor it would place now is owed, and placed by the next flush unless $sl_curat places it first (a page writes about 90 statements a key)
            put.skip = 0
            if scr<SL.S.DIFF> + 0 >= 1 then
               if compare(scr<SL.S.TERM>, ctx<SL.S.TERM>) = 0 and compare(scr<SL.S.TATTR>, ctx<SL.S.TATTR>) = 0 then put.skip = 1
            end
            ctx = scr
            ctx<SL.S.DIFF> = 0
            cq.last = 'put'
            if put.skip then
               cq.owed = 1
            end else
               cq.owed = 0
               gosub put.cursor
            end
         end

      case op = 'flush'
         if ctx.open then gosub flush

      case op = 'unsync'
         if ctx.open then
            ss.lost = 1
            ctx<SL.S.SYNC> = 0
         end

      case op = 'refresh'
         if ctx.open and ctx<SL.S.TK> then
            ss.lost = 1
            ctx<SL.S.SYNC> = 0
         end

      case op = 'toolkit'
         if ctx.open then ctx<SL.S.TK> = 1

      case op = 'open'
         gosub blank
         blank.scr<SL.S.OWNED> = 1
         for r = 0 to @crthigh - 1
            blank.scr<SL.S.TERM, r + 1> = space(@crtwide)       ;* S.113: the terminal's picture; SL.S.SYNC says whether it can be believed
            blank.scr<SL.S.TATTR, r + 1> = str('n', @crtwide)
         next r
         ctx = blank.scr
         ctx.open = 1
         scr = ctx
         rz.pending = 0                         ;* a resize from before this screen is not this program's
         ss.lost = 0

      case op = 'resized'
         scr = ctx.resized + 0                  ;* 1 if the context has been made a new size since this was last asked
         ctx.resized = 0

      case op = 'pending'
         scr = 0
         if rz.pending then scr = 1             ;* a resize handed out by $sl_key and not yet delivered by the program's wait
         rz.pending = 0

      case op = 'close'
         ctx = ''
         ctx.open = 0
         ctx.resized = 0
         rz.pending = 0
         ss.lost = 0
   end case

   return

* The terminal is not the size the kept context was made for (the window was resized, or TERM w,h was typed): $sl_resize
* makes it the new size, keeping every cell that still fits, and draws the screen again from it.  What was drawn with PRINT
* or CRT is not in the shadow, and goes, as for SCREEN REFRESH.
* S.113: a page of the toolkit draws its whole layout again for the key resize, so its screen is not drawn here as well: the context is
* made the new size, what the terminal shows is not known (a terminal may cut, pad or reflow it), and the next flush repaints it once.
resize:
   if ctx<SL.S.TK> then
      call $sl_resize(ctx, 'defer')
      ctx<SL.S.SYNC> = 0 ; ss.lost = 1           ;* MUTANT-POINT resize
   end else
      call $sl_resize(ctx)
   end
   ctx.resized = 1
   return

* S.113: the terminal is brought up to the kept context.  Not known: cleared and drawn whole from the shadow, once.  Known, on a page of the
* toolkit: the rows whose shadow is not what the terminal shows (a SCREEN CLEAR that nothing has been drawn over since) have the cells that
* differ written.  The clip is lifted for it (the whole screen is the terminal's), and the cursor is put back where the program left it.
flush:
   if not(ctx<SL.S.OWNED>) then return
   fl.all = ss.lost
   if ctx<SL.S.TK> and ctx<SL.S.SYNC> # 1 then fl.all = 1
   if not(fl.all) and not(ctx<SL.S.TK>) then return
   fl.cur = ''
   call $sl_curat('get', fl.cur)
   fl.clip = ctx<SL.S.CLIP>
   ctx<SL.S.CLIP> = ''
   fl.wrote = 0
   if fl.all then
      ctx<SL.S.DIFF> = 0
      call $sl_repaint(ctx)                     ;* sets SL.S.SYNC and the terminal's picture
      ss.lost = 0
      fl.wrote = 1
   end else
      fl.w = ctx<SL.S.W> + 0
      fl.last = ctx<SL.S.H> - 1
      ctx<SL.S.DIFF> = 1
      for fr = 0 to fl.last
         ft = ctx<SL.S.SHADOW, fr + 1>
         fa = ctx<SL.S.ATTRS, fr + 1>
         if compare(ft, ctx<SL.S.TERM, fr + 1>) # 0 or compare(fa, ctx<SL.S.TATTR, fr + 1>) # 0 then
            if fr = fl.last and not(terminfo('xenl')) then
               ft = ft[1, fl.w - 1]             ;* the corner cell would scroll the terminal
               fa = fa[1, fl.w - 1]
            end
            call $sl_put(ctx, 0, fr, ft, fa)
            fl.wrote = 1
         end
      next fr
      ctx<SL.S.DIFF> = 0
   end
   ctx<SL.S.CLIP> = fl.clip
   * the cursor where the last to place it wanted it: a put (its own, or one that was owed) or $sl_curat
   if fl.wrote or cq.owed then
      if cq.last = 'curat' and fl.cur<1, 1> + 0 then
         call $sl_curat('back', fl.cur)
      end else
         gosub put.cursor
         cq.last = 'put'
      end
      cq.owed = 0
   end
   return

* The terminal's cursor where the context says, shown or hidden as it says (what put has always written)
put.cursor:
   if ctx<SL.S.CURON> then
      display @(-31) : @(ctx<SL.S.CUR, 1>, ctx<SL.S.CUR, 2>) :
   end else
      display @(ctx<SL.S.CUR, 1>, ctx<SL.S.CUR, 2>) : @(-32) :
   end
   return

blank:
   * A context of the terminal's size, every cell blank and style n
   blank.scr = ''
   blank.scr<SL.S.W> = @crtwide
   blank.scr<SL.S.H> = @crthigh
   blank.scr<SL.S.CUR> = 0 : @vm : 0
   blank.scr<SL.S.CURON> = 1
   blank.scr<SL.S.OWNED> = 0
   for r = 0 to @crthigh - 1
      blank.scr<SL.S.SHADOW, r + 1> = space(@crtwide)
      blank.scr<SL.S.ATTRS, r + 1> = str('n', @crtwide)
   next r
   return
end

* END-CODE
